														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2103958">
									

<p>The problem is the sheer timescale involved - the average dog life span is around 10 years, give or take a few, but mostly give. It would likely leave the people with very inconsistent memories and a good deal of cognitive dissonance - I mean, think of petting your dog and like, not being able to escape the pervasive thought he used to be bigger and somewhat paler in colour.</p>
<p>That said, this just about works as a misguided effort on part of the site director and actually makes it creepier. Not the least, my parents apparently did this to me once with a hampster when I was about 5 or so - quietly replaced the one that had died after he's escaped. I noticed a number of differences, mainly that his slightly lame leg had healed, but with a bit of nudging from them I ascribed it to the grasshoppers I've been feeding him that summer having such a positive effect.</p>

							</div>

								
					
